{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Species‐composition similarity between reef pairs declines with increasing geographic distance (distance‐decay effect).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify and model the decay of community similarity with geographic distance among reefs.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef (id_reef), aggregate the full species list observed across all years.  \n2. Compute pairwise Jaccard similarity indices between species‐sets of all reef pairs.  \n3. Extract latitude and longitude for each reef and compute great‐circle distances (km) between all pairs.  \n4. Fit a nonlinear decay model (e.g. similarity ~ exp(−beta * distance)) or a linear regression of log(similarity) vs distance.  \n5. Test if the distance coefficient differs significantly from zero.  \n6. Visualize the distance‐decay curve: similarity vs distance with fitted decay line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Distance matrix of pairwise Jaccard similarities and distances.  \n2. Model summary showing estimated decay parameter and its significance.  \n3. Plot of similarity vs distance with fitted decay curve and confidence band."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs experiencing faster long‐term warming (higher SST trend) show stronger declines in species richness over time.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether the rate of increase in mean SST at each reef predicts its trend in species richness.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef, compute yearly mean_sst and species_richness (unique species count per reef‐year).  \n2. Fit a linear regression of mean_sst ~ year for each reef to estimate SST trend (slope_SST).  \n3. Fit a linear regression of species_richness ~ year for each reef to estimate richness trend (slope_richness).  \n4. Merge slopes into a single table (one row per reef).  \n5. Fit a correlation test or regression: slope_richness ~ slope_SST.  \n6. Plot slope_richness vs slope_SST with regression line and confidence interval.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of reef‐level slopes for SST and richness.  \n2. Regression summary testing the relationship between SST slope and richness slope.  \n3. Scatterplot of richness‐trend vs SST‐trend with fitted line."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Year‐to‐year species turnover within reefs (beta diversity) is highest at mid‐latitudes compared to extreme north or south reefs (unimodal turnover–latitude relationship).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether temporal turnover in species composition peaks at intermediate latitudes.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef and for each pair of consecutive survey years, compute Jaccard dissimilarity between species lists.  \n2. Calculate mean annual turnover (mean dissimilarity) per reef.  \n3. Extract fixed latitude degree per reef and compute degree^2.  \n4. Fit a regression: turnover ~ degree + I(degree^2).  \n5. Test the significance and sign of the quadratic term (expect positive curvature).  \n6. Plot mean turnover vs latitude with quadratic fit line and 95% CI.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Reef‐level table of mean turnover and latitude.  \n2. Regression output for linear and quadratic latitude terms.  \n3. Plot of turnover vs latitude with fitted unimodal curve."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Within‐reef spatial heterogeneity in biomass density (standard deviation across transects per year) decreases as mean SST increases.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine if warmer conditions homogenize biomass density among transects within the same reef and year.",  
      "steps":"1. For each transect observation, compute biomass_density = biomass / (area/10000).  \n2. Group by reef‐year and calculate SD(biomass_density) and mean SST.  \n3. Fit a mixed‐effects model: sd_biomass_density ~ mean_sst + (1|id_reef).  \n4. Test if the SST coefficient is negative and significant.  \n5. Plot sd_biomass_density vs mean_sst with fitted regression line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Reef‐year dataset with SD of biomass density and mean SST.  \n2. Mixed‐model summary for SST effect on heterogeneity.  \n3. Scatterplot of heterogeneity vs SST with regression line."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Biomass density shows a non‐linear (quadratic) relationship with survey depth, peaking at intermediate depths.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for a hump‐shaped depth effect on biomass density across transects.",  
      "steps":"1. For each transect compute biomass_density as above.  \n2. Create depth^2 term.  \n3. Fit a mixed‐effects model: biomass_density ~ depth + I(depth^2) + (1|id_reef).  \n4. Test significance of the quadratic depth term and estimate the depth at which predicted biomass is maximal.  \n5. Plot predicted biomass_density vs depth with the quadratic curve and 95% CI.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Model summary with depth and depth^2 coefficients.  \n2. Estimated optimum depth for maximum biomass density.  \n3. Plot of biomass_density vs depth with fitted hump‐shaped curve."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs can be partitioned into distinct ecological clusters based on their long‐term average biomass density and mean SST, and these clusters align with particular latitudinal ranges.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Use unsupervised clustering to identify reef types and test their spatial organization by latitude.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef, compute overall mean biomass_density and mean_sst across all transects and years.  \n2. Standardize both variables.  \n3. Determine optimal number of clusters (e.g. using elbow method) and perform k‐means clustering.  \n4. Assign cluster labels to reefs and extract their latitude values.  \n5. Test whether cluster means of latitude differ significantly (ANOVA or Kruskal–Wallis).  \n6. Map clusters by geographic location (scatter of longitude vs latitude colored by cluster).",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of reef‐level mean biomass_density, mean_sst, and cluster assignment.  \n2. Plot of clusters on latitude axis (boxplots) and on map.  \n3. Statistical test summary for cluster–latitude association."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reef‐years with two depth strata support higher species richness than those with only one depth stratum.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Examine the effect of number of surveyed depth strata per reef‐year on species richness.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef‐year, count unique depth values observed (strata_count).  \n2. Compute species_richness per reef‐year.  \n3. Categorize reef‐years into strata_count=1 vs strata_count=2.  \n4. Fit a mixed model: species_richness ~ strata_count + (1|id_reef).  \n5. Test if strata_count=2 is associated with significantly higher richness.  \n6. Plot species_richness distributions by strata_count categories.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Reef‐year dataset with strata_count and richness.  \n2. Mixed‐model summary testing strata_count effect.  \n3. Boxplot of richness by strata_count."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Incorporating a spatial autocorrelation term into mixed‐effects models of biomass density significantly improves model fit and reduces residual spatial clustering.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare biomass density models with and without explicit spatial structure to quantify improvements in fit and residual autocorrelation.",  
      "steps":"1. Compute biomass_density for each transect.  \n2. Fit Model A: biomass_density ~ depth + mean_sst + (1|id_reef).  \n3. Fit Model B: same fixed effects and random effect plus a spatial covariance structure (e.g. Gaussian process based on reef coordinates or a spatially structured random effect).  \n4. Compare Model A vs Model B using AIC, BIC, and likelihood‐ratio test.  \n5. For each model, compute Moran’s I on reef‐level aggregated residuals.  \n6. Summarize improvement in model fit and reduction in spatial autocorrelation.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Model summaries and fit statistics (AIC, BIC, log‐likelihood) for both models.  \n2. Moran’s I values and p‐values for residuals from both models.  \n3. Table or text summarizing which model performs better and how spatial autocorrelation is reduced."  
    }  
  }  
]}
